      {
        "id": 217749,
        "name": "Escher",
        "username": "Escher",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/escher/{size}/4865_2.png",
        "created_at": "2018-05-04T12:39:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"TakeWalker\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/takewalker/48/5677_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> TakeWalker:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>But it&nbsp;<em>does</em> mean \"magic eater\" in Welsh!</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Well… at least, it combines the word for <em>magic </em>and the word for <em>eat</em>.&nbsp; Putting them together that way is probably totally ungrammatical if you asked an actual welsh speaker. I think <em>hud-bwytawr</em> would be more accurate (magic-eater rather than magic-eat), but I'm not sure.&nbsp; And the B might turn into an F in that context… or maybe an M… I don't know the rules for consonant mutation in Welsh.</p>\n<p>For those who don't know Welsh, W and Y are vowels. W sounds like \"oo\", so <em>cwm</em> ('valley') is pronounced like 'koom'.&nbsp; Y is… complicated.&nbsp; Their pronunciation 'hud-BOY-ta' was pretty much correct.</p>",
        "post_number": 25,
        "post_type": 1,
